MERGE (d:Decision {node_id: 'decision-far-0002-auth'})
SET d.project = 'maverick-dev-friend',
    d.label = 'FAR-0002-auth: FAR-0002-auth: Permission Storage Ownership — Hybrid (Keycloak IdP + Postgres RBAC)',
    d.rationale = '- **Input:** P-05 requires every permission mutation in `audit_logs` with `SET LOCAL actor` → **Implication:** Keycloak admin-console events do NOT flow into Maverick `audit_logs`, so Option A fails this goal outright. Option C keeps mutations inside the Postgres database — expressed as PostgREST table writes for CRUD or as `CREATE FUNCTION` RPCs exposed via `POST /rest/v1/rpc/<name>` for multi-step logic (per P-02 v1.1.0 + `postgrest-rpc-pattern` skill) — where the `capture_audit()` trigger and `emit_domain_event()` outbox fire automatically. `SET LOCAL app.current_subject` is populated by Hono middleware before handing the JWT off to PostgREST so the audit row carries the actor regardless of whether the mutation is a table write or an RPC call.\n- **Input:** P-13 mandates `fund_id` is extracted from the Keycloak JWT claim, never from headers/query/body/post-facto DB lookups → **Implication:** Option B\'s `fund_memberships` table becomes the de-facto source of `fund_id`, which departs from P-13\'s literal reading; Option C preserves JWT-claim extraction because Keycloak still emits `fund_id`.\n- **Input:** P-01 RLS policies need to reference the actor\'s role/permission in SQL (not just `fund_id`) → **Implication:** Option A\'s JWT-only permission scopes can\'t be joined in RLS predicates; Option C stores permissions in Postgres where RLS policies can reference them directly.\n- **Input:** P-07 `requirePermission()` runs on every mutation request and must be cache-backed per P-08 → **Implication:** Local Postgres + Redis is hot-path-friendly; Option A\'s Keycloak token-introspection or scope decode paths add latency or couple request performance to Keycloak availability.\n- **Input:** Fund admins must manage member permissions via a Maverick product UI (goal 7) → **Implication:** Option C\'s permission tables are writable via PostgREST — either direct table writes against `user_roles` / `fund_permission_overrides` with RLS + `has_permission()` enforcing authz, or `POST /rest/v1/rpc/<name>` RPC functions for multi-step admin workflows. Authorization lives entirely in RLS predicates (`has_permission()` SQL function from T027) rather than in `requirePermission()` Hono middleware — there is no mutation-path Hono middleware to bypass. Option A would force Keycloak-admin access for every role change and lose the trigger-fed audit trail.\n\n---',
    d.status = 'Stale',
    d.declared_at = datetime();
